.  Its second point \\(B_{n-k+u}\\) equals\n     \\((n-k+u,\\;n-k+u-1)\\).  Any point \\((a,m)\\) with the same row \\(m=n-k+u\\) and\n     column \\(a\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n+1-m=n+1-(n-k+u)=k+1-u\\).  Since\n     \\(k+1-u\\ge1\\), such a point coincides with \\(B_{n-k+u}\\) when \\(a=k+1-u\\); all\n     other points of that row are already covered by the horizontal line\n     \\(H_{m-1}=H_{n-k+u-1}\\) (which exists because \\(m-1\\le n-k\\)).  Hence every\n     point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n   Consequently the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies both required conditions and\n   contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 together with the counting argument yields the necessary condition\n   \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that **every** integer\n   \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) is\n   attainable.  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly this interval.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nAny sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most two points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line contain two distinct points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in S_n\\) with\n\\(x_2>x_1\\).  Because both points have integer coordinates, the slope is a rational\nnumber \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\\frac{p}{q}\\) where\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\), \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), and \\(q>0\\).\nAll integer points of the line are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_1+qt,\\;y_1+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb